        "title": "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "97f8f938-0d50-59f5-922b-f7bea8bb44f7",
        ":question/problem": "Jason, a biologist, is measuring the growth of bacteria in a Petri dish by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of bacteria in the dish.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bacteria in the dish is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of bacteria inside the dish after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in\u00a0}N}{\\text{Change in\u00a0}t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{\u2032}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bacteria is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${N}^{\u2032}(t)=r(t).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "874c5434-9157-538e-be59-176244d35d61",
            ":answer-field/key": "selection",
