  {
    "label": "c00a28ec-ae50-52ff-8f5b-35e535d7e4c4",
    "record": {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186064272,
        "id": "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782",
        "title": "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c00a28ec-ae50-52ff-8f5b-35e535d7e4c4",
        ":question/problem": "To measure the success of an amusement park, Ed uses the following three functions that model the daily number of visitors to the park $t$ minutes after the park is opened to the public.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the park\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the park\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the park\n\nWhat is the relationship between the functions above?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in\u00a0}N}{\\text{Change in\u00a0}t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{\u2032}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the park changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the park at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{\u2032}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "1ee88502-7069-5e01-a8d0-c684a215ef26",
            ":answer-field/key": "selection",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
