        ":question/problem": "A model records the mass of grain in a silo at time $t$, measured in hours after observations begin.\n\n- $E(t)$ - the mass of grain in a silo, in kilograms.\n\n- $A(t)$ - the positive rate at which the amount is added, in kilograms per hour.\n\n- $L(t)$ - the signed contribution of removal to the rate of change, in kilograms per hour; $L(t)<0$ when removal occurs.\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The function $E(t)$ gives the mass of grain in a silo after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}E(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }E}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $E'(t)$ gives the instantaneous rate at which the amount changes at time $t$.\n\nIn addition, $L(t)$ already includes the negative sign of removal. We add the two signed contributions at time $t$. That is,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=A(t)+L(t).\n$$\n\nThe change ratio denotes an instantaneous rate: the limit of change ratios as the time interval tends to zero.",
